\title{Local Trust Regions & Functional State}

\subtree{
  \title{Multi-Region Search & Asynchronous Evaluation}

  \p{
    Over high-dimensional domains, global uncertainty grows toward box boundaries, pulling unconstrained acquisition away from promising basins. [baux.regions](docs.html#baux.regions) maintains #{R} local axis-aligned trust regions #{\{T_r(x_{0,r}, \ell_r, g_r)\}_{r=1}^R} centered at regional incumbents #{x_{0,r}} with side lengths #{\ell_r} and restart generation #{g_r} (Eriksson et al. [[EPG+19]](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.01739), Daulton et al. [[DEB+22]](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v180/daulton22a.html)). Consecutive improvements double #{\ell_r}, consecutive stalls halve #{\ell_r} down to a restart threshold, and candidate pools perturb sparse coordinate subsets around #{x_{0,r}} when #{D} is large. On multi-objective problems (#{M \ge 2}), each region scalarizes normalized outputs with simplex weights [[DEB+22]](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v180/daulton22a.html).
  }

  \p{
    Cross-region incumbent updates follow one of two rules: \code{sharing="origin_only"} updates #{x_{0,r}} only from evaluations proposed by region #{r}, whereas \code{sharing="ellipsoid_ball"} adopts any higher-scoring global observation inside the Euclidean ball circumscribing #{T_r}'s box in solver coordinates. When evaluations run asynchronously, each proposed arm carries an \code{AskId} ticket #{(r, g, a)} recording its originating region #{r}, dispatch generation #{g = g_r}, and slot #{a}. Out-of-order completions via [baux.complete](docs.html#baux.complete) always append observations to #{\mathcal{D}}, while side-length updates on #{T_r} apply only when #{g = g_r} so delayed evaluations from before a restart do not alter the re-centered region. [baux.cancel](docs.html#baux.cancel) releases abandoned slots without modifying #{\mathcal{D}}.
  }
}

\subtree{
  \title{Deterministic Rollouts & Checkpointing}

  \p{
    Each [\code{OptimizerState}](docs.html#baux.OptimizerState) pairs observation arrays and regional trust-region state with a splittable 128-bit PRNG key (Claessen & Pałka [[CP13]](https://doi.org/10.1145/2503778.2503784), Steele, Lea, & Flood [[SLF14]](https://doi.org/10.1145/2660193.2660195)). Sequential loops via [baux.scan](docs.html#baux.scan) and pre-split parallel branches #{k \mapsto (k_1, \dots, k_B)} via [baux.pmap](docs.html#baux.pmap) produce deterministic trajectories independent of worker thread scheduling.
  }

  \p{
    [baux.freeze](docs.html#baux.freeze) and [baux.thaw](docs.html#baux.thaw) serialize the full search state—observations, surrogate fit state, trust-region side lengths and success/failure counters, pending asynchronous tickets, and PRNG state—into an authenticated binary snapshot summarized by [baux.digest](docs.html#baux.digest). Restoring against a domain-compatible template state #{s_0} satisfies #{\mathrm{thaw}(s_0, \mathrm{freeze}(s)) = s}, so resumed runs propose identical candidate batches and parallel rollouts:
  }

  \pre{
import numpy as np
import baux

bounds = np.tile([-3.0, 3.0], (8, 1))
weights = np.geomspace(1.0, 16.0, 8)


def anisotropic_loss(x):
    return -np.sum(weights * (x - 0.35) ** 2 + 0.1 * np.cos(3.0 * x), axis=1)


pipe = (
    baux.lhd(8)
    | baux.enn(k=6)
    | baux.thompson()
    | baux.regions(count=4, sharing="ellipsoid_ball")
)
s0 = baux.init(bounds, pipe, key=baux.key(101))
s_warm, b_init = baux.ask(s0, num_arms=8)
s_warm = baux.complete(s_warm, b_init.ids, anisotropic_loss(b_init.points))

s_live, b1 = baux.ask(s_warm, num_arms=2)
s_live, b2 = baux.ask(s_live, num_arms=2)
s_live = baux.complete(s_live, [b2.ids[0]], anisotropic_loss(b2.points[:1]))
s_live = baux.cancel(s_live, [b2.ids[1]])
s_live = baux.complete(s_live, b1.ids, anisotropic_loss(b1.points))

blob = baux.freeze(s_live)
restored_a = baux.thaw(s0, blob)
restored_b = baux.thaw(s0, baux.freeze(restored_a))
assert baux.freeze(restored_a) == blob
assert baux.digest(s_live) == baux.digest(restored_a) == baux.digest(restored_b)

rollouts_live = baux.pmap(
    s_live,
    baux.key(77),
    branches=4,
    branch_fn=lambda i, st, k: baux.digest(
        baux.scan(st, k, steps=4, eval_fn=anisotropic_loss, num_arms=2)[0]
    ),
)
rollouts_a = baux.pmap(
    restored_a,
    baux.key(77),
    branches=4,
    branch_fn=lambda i, st, k: baux.digest(
        baux.scan(st, k, steps=4, eval_fn=anisotropic_loss, num_arms=2)[0]
    ),
)
rollouts_b = baux.pmap(
    restored_b,
    baux.key(77),
    branches=4,
    branch_fn=lambda i, st, k: baux.digest(
        baux.scan(st, k, steps=4, eval_fn=anisotropic_loss, num_arms=2)[0]
    ),
)
assert rollouts_live == rollouts_a == rollouts_b and len(set(rollouts_live)) == 4
  }
}
